Egypt, who had fought against Russia and Napoleon's army in Egypt. He lost the Battle of Abukir in 1799. Mustafa Pasha was an ethnic Albanian.", "newValue": "Mustafa Paşa, an Ottoman commander of Albanian ethnicity born in Edirne, played a pivotal role in the defense of Ottoman Egypt against both Russian expansion and Napoleon Bonaparte's French expedition in the late 18th century. As the paternal uncle of Muhammad Ali of Egypt, his military career and presence in the crucial province of Egypt positioned him at the nexus of imperial decline and emergent regional power. His involvement in the tumultuous period of the Napoleonic Wars in the Near East underscored the Ottoman Empire's struggle to maintain control over its distant territories.

His most notable military engagement occurred during Napoleon's Egyptian Campaign. In 1799, Mustafa Paşa commanded the Ottoman land forces at the Battle of Abukir, where his army suffered a decisive defeat at the hands of Napoleon's forces. This crushing loss, which saw thousands of Ottoman soldiers killed, wounded, or captured, significantly diminished the Sultan's capacity to repel the French invasion and marked a critical moment in the campaign. Despite this setback, his earlier efforts against Russian incursions demonstrated his commitment to Ottoman territorial integrity.

